## Using result in Postconditions

`result` represents the value that the function returns.

### Simple Return Value Checks
```xiom
fn double(n: Int) -> Int
  ensures: result == n * 2;
  ensures: result % 2 == 0;
{ return n * 2; }
```

### Range Guarantees
```xiom
fn clamp(value: Int, min: Int, max: Int) -> Int
  requires: min <= max;
  ensures: result >= min;
  ensures: result <= max;
{ if value < min { return min; } if value > max { return max; } return value; }
```

### Relating result to Parameters
```xiom
fn max_of_three(a: Int, b: Int, c: Int) -> Int
  ensures: result >= a && result >= b && result >= c;
  ensures: result == a || result == b || result == c;
{ var m = a; if b > m { m = b; } if c > m { m = c; } return m; }
```

### result with Generic Types
```xiom
fn clone[T: Clone](value: &T) -> T
  ensures: result == value;
{ return value.clone(); }
```

### Mathematical Functions
```xiom
fn factorial(n: Int) -> Int
  requires: n >= 0;
  ensures: result > 0;
{ if n <= 1 { return 1; } return n * factorial(n - 1); }
```

syntax_ref:
fn name(params) -> ReturnType
  ensures: result > 0;
  ensures: result == some_computation;
{ return value; }

